      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded 1794 as Redding, renamed Elizabethtown in 1794, then Elizabeth City in 1801 after Elizabeth Tooley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Dismal Swamp Canal turned Elizabeth City into a mercantile hub in its early years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1881 the Elizabeth City and Norfolk Railroad began, later becoming part of Norfolk Southern, shifting trade away from the waterfront.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Museum of the Albemarle sits on the waterfront, showcasing the region’s history and culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Elizabeth City State University, founded in 1891, is part of the UNC system and hosts the College of the Albemarle.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_City%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Known as the “Harbor of Hospitality,” Elizabeth City’s location at a bend of the Pasquotank River made it a historic shipping center.",
